West Africa: The Forgotten Subcontinent

Toward a Geographic, Historical, Demographic, and Civilizational Reclassification of Western Africa

Abstract

The conventional geographical classification of Africa places West Africa within the broad continental category of Africa and generally treats it as a subregional subdivision comparable to North, East, Central, and Southern Africa. This paper proposes a different analytical framework: that West Africa can reasonably be conceptualized as a subcontinent within Africa, analogous in certain respects to the Indian subcontinent within Asia.

The argument does not depend upon claiming that West Africa is physically separated from the remainder of Africa, nor does it propose that the region constitutes a separate continent. Instead, it examines whether West Africa possesses sufficient geographic scale, ecological differentiation, historical continuity, demographic magnitude, cultural interaction, economic integration, and institutional coherence to justify subcontinental status as an analytical category.

The paper develops a multidimensional model of subcontinentality based on five variables: geography, historical continuity, cultural interconnectedness, demographic scale, and economic-institutional integration. It then applies this framework to West Africa and compares the resulting characteristics with those commonly associated with the Indian subcontinent.

The evidence indicates that West Africa possesses many of the characteristics that make the subcontinent concept useful in South Asian scholarship. Its ecological zones create a recognizable north-south environmental system; its major rivers and trade corridors historically connected the Sahel, savanna, forest, and Atlantic coast; its precolonial history includes successive large-scale political formations such as Ghana, Mali, and Songhai; and its modern societies remain interconnected through migration, trade, language, culture, and regional institutions.

The paper concludes that “West African subcontinent” should be considered a legitimate analytical concept in geography, history, political economy, and futures studies, even though it is not presently a universally recognized formal geographical classification. Such a reframing could provide a more appropriate scale for understanding West Africa’s history, demographic transformation, urbanization, economic integration, and future geopolitical significance.

Keywords: West Africa, subcontinent, geography, African history, Mali Empire, Songhai Empire, Ghana Empire, ECOWAS, regional integration, political geography, demography, civilization, futures studies

