1. Introduction

Geographical categories appear objective, but many of them are ultimately intellectual constructions.

The world is conventionally divided into continents and subregions: Asia, Africa, Europe, North America, South America, Antarctica, and Australia/Oceania. These categories are useful, but they are not perfectly uniform. Within continents, certain regions possess such distinctive physical geography, historical trajectories, demographic structures, and cultural systems that scholars routinely employ additional concepts such as subcontinent, macroregion, civilizational region, or world region.

The most familiar example is the Indian subcontinent.

The Indian subcontinent is physically connected to the larger Eurasian landmass, yet its distinctive geography, including its relationship to the Himalayas, the Indian Ocean, the Arabian Sea, and the Bay of Bengal, has contributed to its treatment as a major geographic unit within Asia. Contemporary usage often encompasses India, Pakistan, Bangladesh and, depending upon definition, neighboring South Asian territories. (Wikipedia)

The important question, therefore, is not whether a region is physically detached from its continent.

The more interesting question is:

When does a region become sufficiently large, distinctive, interconnected, and historically coherent that the concept of “subcontinent” becomes analytically useful?

This paper applies that question to West Africa.

The central thesis is:

West Africa can legitimately be conceptualized as a subcontinent of Africa because it constitutes a geographically extensive, ecologically differentiated, historically interconnected, demographically significant, culturally dynamic, and economically integrated regional system.

This proposition should not be misunderstood.

The argument is not that West Africa is literally a separate continent.

Nor is it an argument that every society in West Africa shares one culture.

Nor is it an argument that contemporary national borders should disappear.

Rather, it proposes that the analytical scale of “West Africa” may be too important to be understood merely as one administrative subdivision of the African continent.

West Africa has repeatedly functioned as a large-scale historical system.

Its empires controlled enormous territories.

Its trade networks crossed ecological boundaries.

Its rivers created corridors connecting different societies.

Its peoples migrated across political boundaries.

Its languages interacted for centuries.

Its religious networks extended across the Sahara and Atlantic world.

And in the modern period, regional organizations have explicitly attempted to institutionalize economic and political interdependence.

The question is therefore not whether West Africa has unity in the sense of homogeneity.

It does not.

The question is whether it possesses systemic unity despite internal diversity.

This paper argues that it does.

