3. The Geographic Case for West African Subcontinentality

3.1 Scale

One of the strongest arguments concerns geographic scale.

The geographic conception of West Africa varies according to the institution and analytical purpose. The OECD has used a broad West African regional framework encompassing the ECOWAS zone together with Cameroon, Chad, and Mauritania because of their historical, demographic, and cultural relationships. That broader region was described as covering approximately 7.9 million square kilometers. (OECD)

This is an enormous geographical system.

The important point is not that every possible definition of West Africa produces exactly the same boundary.

It does not.

Instead, the variation itself illustrates the existence of a recognizable macroregion whose boundaries are sufficiently meaningful to support independent geographic analysis.

A region approaching continental scale should not automatically be conceptualized as a mere administrative subdivision.

⸻

3.2 Ecological Diversity

West Africa contains a remarkable sequence of ecological environments.

Moving broadly from north to south, one encounters:

1. the Sahara,
2. the Sahel,
3. Sudanian savanna,
4. Guinean savanna,
5. tropical forest,
6. coastal ecosystems,
7. mangrove systems,
8. Atlantic maritime environments.

These environments are not isolated.

They form an interconnected ecological gradient.

The OECD describes West Africa as containing a succession of environmental zones extending from the Sahara through the Sahel and Sudanian areas toward the forests of Guinea. These zones possess ecological complementarities that historically contributed to trade and population organization. (OECD)

This is a critical feature of the subcontinental argument.

West Africa is not simply a collection of countries occupying adjacent land.

It is an ecological system.

The desert produces commodities and trade requirements different from those of the savanna.

The savanna supports different agricultural and pastoral systems from the forest.

The forest zone possesses different agricultural and ecological characteristics from the Sahel.

The coast connects the entire system to the Atlantic.

Consequently, West Africa’s internal diversity generates economic complementarity.

This is precisely the type of large-scale geographical interaction that can make a subcontinental concept useful.

⸻

4. Rivers as the Architecture of a Subcontinent

Geography is not merely about land.

It is also about movement.

The Niger River is particularly important in this regard.

The Niger creates a major north-south and east-west system of ecological and economic interaction. Cities such as Djenné, Timbuktu, and Gao developed within networks connecting agricultural regions, desert trade routes, and commercial centers.

UNESCO describes Djenné as a major meeting place for traders from the desert regions and the tropical forests of Guinea. Its location near the Niger and Bani rivers helped connect it to Timbuktu and to routes leading toward gold and salt-producing regions. (UNESCO)

This illustrates a deeper principle.

The geography of West Africa historically created connective corridors.

The desert was not simply a barrier.

The Niger was not merely a river.

The Atlantic was not merely a coastline.

Together, these geographic structures created a network through which people, commodities, ideas, religions, technologies, and political power moved.

The region therefore developed as an interconnected geographical system.

⸻

5. The Historical Case: West Africa Before Colonial Borders

Perhaps the strongest argument for conceptualizing West Africa as a subcontinent is historical.

The contemporary political map can obscure the historical geography of the region.

Modern West Africa contains numerous sovereign states whose borders were largely established through colonial processes and subsequent independence settlements.

But the historical West African system was organized differently.

Political power frequently operated across large territories.

Trade networks connected distant ecological zones.

Languages and cultures spread across political boundaries.

States rose and fell without regard to contemporary national frontiers.

The historical geography of West Africa was therefore considerably larger than the modern nation-state map suggests.

⸻

6. Ghana, Mali, and Songhai: Successive Continental-Scale Political Systems

The succession of the Ghana, Mali, and Songhai empires is central to the argument.

Historians have long identified these states as major political formations of medieval West Africa. Cambridge scholarship describes the medieval history of West Africa as being strongly framed by the successive emergence of Ghana, Mali, and Songhay. (Cambridge University Press)

These empires were not miniature kingdoms.

They were extensive political systems connected to major commercial networks.

The Mali Empire, for example, expanded from the Atlantic region toward the interior and Sahara. Historical reconstructions place its maximum extent across large portions of what are now several West African countries. (South African History Online)

The Songhai Empire subsequently became an even larger political formation.

OpenStax describes Songhai under Askia Muhammad as stretching from Kano in Hausaland toward Taghaza and westward toward Senegal, with extensive control over major Saharan trade routes. (OpenStax)

UNESCO’s description of the Tomb of Askia similarly emphasizes Songhai’s control over trans-Saharan trade, particularly the salt and gold trade, and describes the empire as a powerful political formation spanning West Africa during the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. (UNESCO World Heritage Centre)

This succession matters.

The argument is not merely that West Africa once had powerful states.

Many regions have had powerful states.

The more significant observation is that successive states repeatedly operated across a recognizable West African macroregion.

Ghana was followed by Mali.

Mali was followed by Songhai.

Political centers shifted.

Territories changed.

Dynasties changed.

But the underlying regional system persisted.

That is evidence of historical continuity.

⸻

7. Trade as the Integrating Mechanism

The empires of West Africa were deeply connected to trade.

Gold, salt, agricultural products, textiles, livestock, kola nuts, ivory, metals, and other commodities circulated through extensive networks.

West African trade routes linked the forest and savanna to the Sahel and connected the region to North Africa and the Mediterranean world.

OpenStax describes West African trade networks as extending across North Africa and connecting West Africa to the Mediterranean, Europe, the Near East, and beyond. (OpenStax)

This means that West Africa historically occupied an important position within an intercontinental economic system.

The region was not isolated.

It was a major node between:

[
\text{Atlantic Coast}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Forest Zone}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Savanna}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Sahel}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Sahara}
\leftrightarrow
\text{North Africa}
]

This network effectively produced a large-scale regional economy before the emergence of modern nation-states.

