8. Cities as Nodes of the West African System

A subcontinent requires more than territory.

It requires centers of interaction.

West Africa developed numerous urban nodes that performed this function.

Timbuktu, Gao, Djenné, Kano, and other cities became centers of trade, scholarship, religion, administration, and cultural exchange.

Djenné, for example, developed as a commercial meeting point between desert and forest trading systems. (UNESCO)

Gao became a major political and commercial center under Songhai.

Timbuktu became associated with scholarship and long-distance commerce.

Kano emerged as an important center within Hausaland.

These cities should not be understood merely as isolated historical curiosities.

They constituted nodes in a regional network.

A network interpretation of West African history therefore reveals something that a country-by-country interpretation can obscure:

West African civilization was produced through interaction between ecological zones and urban nodes.

