9. Cultural Interconnectedness Without Cultural Uniformity

One possible objection to the subcontinent thesis is cultural diversity.

West Africa contains hundreds of languages and numerous cultural traditions.

But this does not invalidate the subcontinental argument.

Indeed, the Indian subcontinent itself is extraordinarily diverse.

Subcontinental identity does not require cultural homogeneity.

A better model is:

[
\text{Subcontinental Identity}

\text{Diversity}
+
\text{Interaction}
+
\text{Historical Continuity}
]

rather than:

[
\text{Subcontinental Identity}

\text{Cultural Uniformity}
]

West Africa satisfies the first equation much better than the second.

Its peoples are diverse, but they have interacted for centuries.

Languages have influenced one another.

Religious traditions have traveled across ethnic boundaries.

Trade has created multilingual communities.

Migration has connected distant regions.

Intermarriage has produced cultural hybridity.

Music, cuisine, clothing, oral traditions, religious practices, and political institutions have circulated across borders.

The result is not one West African culture.

It is a West African cultural system composed of many cultures.

