10. Islam, Indigenous Traditions, and the Formation of Regional Networks

Religion provides another example of regional interconnectedness.

Islam entered West African political and commercial networks through trans-Saharan interactions and became particularly influential among ruling elites, merchants, scholars, and urban populations.

At the same time, Islam interacted with preexisting religious and cultural traditions.

The result was not simple replacement.

Instead, West Africa developed distinctive religious landscapes characterized by interaction between Islamic scholarship, indigenous traditions, political institutions, and local social structures.

The Tomb of Askia provides a material illustration of this synthesis: UNESCO identifies the monument as both Islamic and distinctly West African in architectural form, describing its adaptation of local building traditions to Islamic requirements. (UNESCO World Heritage Centre)

This is precisely the type of cultural synthesis that can generate a regional civilizational identity without requiring uniformity.

