11. The Atlantic Dimension

A modern conception of West Africa must also include the Atlantic.

The region’s history cannot be understood exclusively through the Sahara.

Beginning in the late medieval and early modern periods, Atlantic maritime networks increasingly connected West Africa to Europe and the Americas.

This transformed the economic geography of the region.

West African coastal societies became connected to global systems involving commerce, warfare, migration, colonialism, and eventually the transatlantic slave trade.

The Atlantic therefore became a second major geographic interface.

West Africa can consequently be understood as occupying an unusual position between three enormous systems:

[
\text{Sahara}
\leftrightarrow
\text{West Africa}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Atlantic}
]

This geographic position gives West Africa a distinctive strategic character.

It is simultaneously a Sahelian region, an Atlantic region, and a tropical region.

⸻

12. Colonialism and the Fragmentation of Regional Geography

The colonial period introduced a new political geography.

European powers divided West Africa into colonial territories.

These territories subsequently became independent states.

The result was the creation of national borders that frequently intersected older cultural, linguistic, ecological, and commercial networks.

This produced a paradox.

Politically, West Africa became more fragmented.

Economically and culturally, however, many regional relationships continued.

People continued to migrate.

Markets continued to cross borders.

Languages continued to spread.

Families remained divided across states.

Trade corridors persisted.

Consequently, the political fragmentation of West Africa did not eliminate its underlying regional structure.

Instead, it layered a new system of sovereign states over an older regional network.

This distinction is essential.

The existence of numerous countries does not disprove the existence of a subcontinent.

Europe provides an obvious conceptual example: numerous sovereign states coexist within a recognizable geographical and historical region.

⸻

13. The Modern Institutionalization of West Africa

Perhaps the strongest contemporary evidence for regional coherence is institutional.

The Economic Community of West African States, ECOWAS, was established in 1975 to promote economic integration across the region. Its institutional objectives have included trade, transportation, telecommunications, energy, agriculture, natural resources, monetary and financial cooperation, and social and cultural matters. (ECOWAS)

This is significant because institutions do not emerge in a geographical vacuum.

They attempt to formalize existing relationships and address shared problems.

The ECOWAS project can therefore be interpreted as a modern attempt to construct an institutional architecture corresponding to the underlying regional system.

There has nevertheless been substantial political change.

Burkina Faso, Mali, and Niger formally withdrew from ECOWAS on January 29, 2025. ECOWAS subsequently continued discussions concerning future relations, trade, movement, and regional cooperation with those states. (ECOWAS)

This development should not simply be interpreted as evidence that West African regionalism has failed.

Rather, it demonstrates that West African identity and regional integration operate at different levels.

States may disagree over political institutions while their populations remain connected through geography, trade, migration, language, and history.

That distinction strengthens the subcontinental concept.

A subcontinent does not require one government.

