14. Demographic Scale

Population is another major factor.

The World Bank’s regional data category for Western and Central Africa alone reports a population exceeding 500 million in the mid-2020s. (DataBank)

Although this World Bank classification does not correspond exactly to every definition of West Africa, it demonstrates the enormous demographic scale of the broader western African macroregion.

The demographic importance of West Africa is also intensified by its age structure and urbanization.

Major metropolitan areas—including Lagos, Accra, Abidjan, Dakar, Kano, and others—are becoming increasingly interconnected.

The future of West Africa will therefore be substantially urban.

This creates a new form of subcontinental integration.

Historically, trade routes connected cities.

In the twenty-first century, transportation networks, telecommunications, financial systems, migration, and digital platforms can connect metropolitan regions at unprecedented speed.

The emerging West African urban system may therefore become more important than the individual national economies that comprise it.

