20. The Subcontinent as a Historical System

The term “subcontinent” should therefore be understood not simply as a geological category but as a historical-system category.

West Africa exhibits recurring cycles of integration and fragmentation.

A simplified model might be expressed as:

[
I(t) = P(t) + T(t) + C(t) + U(t)
]

where:

* (P(t)) = political integration,
* (T(t)) = trade integration,
* (C(t)) = cultural integration,
* (U(t)) = urban integration.

At different historical moments, one component becomes stronger than another.

The Mali Empire represented high political integration.

The trans-Saharan trade network represented high economic integration.

Islamic scholarship represented cultural and intellectual integration.

Contemporary metropolitan corridors represent increasingly strong urban integration.

Thus, the precise mechanism of integration changes.

The regional system persists.

