26. Counterarguments

A serious academic argument must acknowledge objections.

26.1 “West Africa is simply a region of Africa.”

This is the conventional classification, and it remains perfectly valid for many purposes.

The argument presented here does not require abandoning the term “region.”

A place can simultaneously be a region and a subcontinent.

The proposal is therefore additive rather than necessarily replacement-oriented.

⸻

26.2 “West Africa lacks the geological separation of India.”

This is perhaps the strongest objection.

The Indian subcontinent has distinctive geological and physiographic characteristics.

West Africa does not reproduce that exact geological situation.

However, the term subcontinent is used in different ways.

If it is defined exclusively through plate tectonics, the West African argument becomes much weaker.

If it is understood more broadly as a large, distinctive geographical-historical unit within a continent, the argument becomes considerably stronger.

The paper therefore proposes a functional-geographical definition of subcontinent, rather than a strictly geological one.

⸻

26.3 “West Africa is too culturally diverse.”

This objection misunderstands the basis of regional identity.

Diversity does not necessarily contradict regional coherence.

Indeed, diversity combined with extensive interaction can be one of the defining characteristics of a large region.

South Asia demonstrates this principle.

So does Europe.

So does West Africa.

⸻

26.4 “Colonial borders prevent meaningful regional unity.”

Colonial borders certainly produced political fragmentation.

But political fragmentation does not erase historical geography.

The persistence of cross-border migration, trade, language communities, family networks, and cultural systems demonstrates that regional relationships continue.

The fact that states have separate governments does not mean they belong to unrelated geographic systems.

⸻

27. Toward a New Geographic Vocabulary

The proposition can therefore be reformulated more cautiously.

Rather than declaring:

“West Africa is officially a subcontinent.”

one could argue:

“West Africa should be recognized as a subcontinental-scale geographic and historical system within Africa.”

This formulation is stronger academically because it does not pretend that the terminology is already universally accepted.

It establishes a conceptual proposal.

The distinction between official classification and analytical classification is crucial.

Academic disciplines routinely introduce new analytical categories when existing ones fail to capture important patterns.

The West African subcontinent concept can serve that purpose.

⸻

28. A West African Subcontinental Model

A mature model would recognize at least seven layers:

Layer 1: Physical geography

Sahara, Sahel, savanna, forest, rivers, coast.

Layer 2: Historical geography

Ghana, Mali, Songhai, Hausa states, Akan states, Yoruba states, Benin and other historical formations.

Layer 3: Cultural geography

Languages, religions, artistic traditions, cuisine, music, oral traditions, social institutions.

Layer 4: Economic geography

Agriculture, mining, trade corridors, ports, financial systems, manufacturing.

Layer 5: Urban geography

Lagos, Accra, Abidjan, Dakar, Kano, Abuja, Lomé, Cotonou, and other metropolitan systems.

Layer 6: Institutional geography

ECOWAS and other regional institutions.

Layer 7: Digital geography

Telecommunications, digital commerce, fintech, online cultural networks, and information flows.

Taken together, these layers describe something much larger than a simple collection of neighboring countries.

They describe a subcontinental system.

