34. Conclusion

West Africa is rarely described as a subcontinent.

Yet when the concept of subcontinent is examined systematically rather than assumed to belong exclusively to South Asia, the West African case becomes compelling.

West Africa possesses enormous geographical scale.

It contains multiple interconnected ecological zones.

Its rivers and trade corridors historically connected distant societies.

It produced major political formations including Ghana, Mali, and Songhai.

Its cities became nodes of transregional commerce and scholarship.

Its peoples have maintained extensive cultural, linguistic, religious, and migratory connections.

Its modern economies remain deeply interconnected.

Its institutions have explicitly sought regional integration.

Its demographic scale is enormous.

And its future challenges increasingly operate at a scale larger than the nation-state.

The argument, therefore, is not that West Africa is secretly another continent.

It is that the conventional hierarchy—

[
\text{Africa}
\rightarrow
\text{West Africa}
\rightarrow
\text{Country}
]

—may be analytically incomplete.

A more sophisticated hierarchy might be:

[
\text{Africa}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{West African Subcontinent}}
\rightarrow
\text{National States}
\rightarrow
\text{Cities and Regions}
]

Under this model, the sovereign states of West Africa remain fully meaningful.

But they become components of a larger geographical and historical system.

The concept also provides a useful corrective to a tendency to interpret Africa primarily through the boundaries of its contemporary states.

West Africa existed as an interconnected human system long before the modern political map.

Ghana, Mali, and Songhai did not recognize today’s borders.

Trade routes did not stop at customs posts.

Rivers did not respect colonial boundaries.

Languages crossed frontiers.

Families migrated.

Religions traveled.

Cities accumulated wealth through connections extending far beyond their immediate surroundings.

Modern regional institutions are, in many respects, attempts to recreate institutionally some of the connectivity that geography and history had already produced.

The subcontinent thesis therefore rests on a simple proposition:

A geographical system should be understood according to the networks that actually connect its people, environments, economies, and histories—not merely according to the political lines drawn across its map.

By that standard, West Africa possesses a powerful claim to subcontinental status.

The phrase “West African Subcontinent” need not replace “West Africa.”

It can deepen it.

It provides a vocabulary for thinking about the region at the scale at which its history operated, its contemporary networks function, and its future may unfold.

The larger intellectual implication is even more important.

Africa should not be conceptualized merely as a single undifferentiated continental unit.

It contains multiple enormous regional systems with their own geographic logics, historical trajectories, cultural networks, and futures.

West Africa is one of the most significant of these.

Calling it a subcontinent is therefore less an attempt to redraw the map than an invitation to redraw the intellectual framework through which the map is understood.

